---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BatteryMark from '../components/BatteryMark.astro';
import LiveCarrierPanel from '../components/demo/LiveCarrierPanel.astro';
import HeroSnippet from '../components/HeroSnippet.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import OsiMark from '../components/OsiMark.astro';
import PlatformStrip from '../components/PlatformStrip.astro';
import TypeScriptMark from '../components/TypeScriptMark.astro';
import { MEASURED_ON, libsignalReadme } from '../lib/comparison.mjs';
import { startupTier } from '../data/pricing.mjs';

const differentiators = [
  {
    title: 'Pure TypeScript, everywhere your app runs',
    /* "No native modules, no prebuild step, no ejecting" was true of the crypto
     * and false of the storage this site's own /product page documents: the
     * encrypted Expo store is expo-sqlite with SQLCipher, which Expo Go does
     * not carry. The limit belongs in the same breath as the capability.
     *
     * Appending the limit was not enough: "no prebuild step" and "needs a
     * development build" stayed in one sentence, and two readers called it a
     * contradiction, because a development build is native project generation
     * however it is spelled. The claim is now scoped the way the approved
     * proof point in docs/messaging.md §5 scopes it — to the *protocol code*,
     * which really is pure TypeScript — rather than to the package as a whole.
     * /product keeps the full reconciliation, where there is room to explain
     * that `npx expo prebuild` is not a command the reader types. */
    body: 'Expo, React Native, browsers, and Node from one package. The protocol code is pure TypeScript with no native crypto module to link — the encrypted Expo store uses SQLCipher, which needs a development build rather than Expo Go.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Post-quantum from the first handshake',
    body: 'PQXDH with ML-KEM establishes the session; the Double Ratchet carries it forward. Not a future roadmap item.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Your backend stays yours',
    body: 'Storage, relay, and encrypted-object adapters are composed by your application. We do not host anything, and nothing phones home.',
  },
  {
    /* The clause here used to be "the work teams usually discover three
       months in". Two fresh readers flagged it independently and both were
       right: "three months" is a statistic, nobody measured it, and there are
       no teams to have measured it on. One wrote "unsourced vendor line —
       which teams? I discount it entirely", the other "a made-up number in a
       page that is otherwise scrupulous about sourcing". That is the exact
       trade the rest of the page refuses — borrowing the authority of a
       measurement without doing one — and it was buying a rhetorical beat.
       The replacement says the same thing as a fact about scope, which any
       reader can check against their own prototype. */
    title: 'The hard parts are included',
    body: 'Multi-device, groups, sealed sender, safety numbers, encrypted attachments and files — the parts a working one-to-one prototype still leaves to build.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Every claim is inspectable',
    body: 'The implementation, the protocol profile, the threat model, and the exact list of metadata the relay still sees are all published.',
  },
  {
    /* This cell said "at a published price" and showed no price, for the whole
     * loop. A fresh reader took the word "published" literally and was right
     * to: "it promises a number that is not on the page and not linked", and
     * they listed it as one of two go/no-go inputs they could not get. The
     * number existed the entire time — /pricing has carried $5,000 since
     * before this loop started, and the header links to it. The page was
     * being coy about the one fact a solo developer decides on.
     *
     * The price is read from the same module /pricing renders, so the two
     * surfaces cannot disagree. Naming the entry tier only, because "starts
     * at" is the honest shape of a three-tier table and the link carries the
     * rest. */
    id: 'agpl-or-commercial',
    title: 'AGPL, or a commercial license',
    body: `The complete SDK is free under AGPL-3.0 — every feature, nothing time-limited. Closed-source products buy annual production rights, from ${startupTier.price} ${startupTier.period} for one product.`,
    link: { href: '/pricing', label: 'See the tiers' },
  },
  /*
   * The band is titled "What you get, and what it costs you to find out" and
   * for eight rounds it listed only the first half. Three fresh readers in two
   * consecutive waves arrived at the missing half unaided and made it their
   * largest gap: one asked what happens to dispute resolution, fraud review
   * and trust-and-safety; one wrote that support messages under E2EE cannot be
   * produced under subpoena, cannot be supervised, and cannot have DLP run
   * over them, and that "the page never names the tradeoff — not to solve it,
   * not even to acknowledge it exists." Their shared conclusion was not that
   * the product is wrong. It is that a page which omits the one cost every
   * evaluator finds in the first meeting has not met an evaluator yet, and
   * that reading is more expensive than the cost itself.
   *
   * It is stated as a consequence of what the relay holds, because that is the
   * part this page can prove: the recorded row two bands down is ciphertext,
   * and everything in this cell follows from that and nothing else. No
   * regulation is named — docs/messaging.md bans "required for HIPAA / GDPR /
   * SOC 2" outright, and the honest form of this cost is the capability a team
   * loses, not a rule someone must obey.
   *
   * The last sentence sends the wrong reader away on purpose. The brief
   * optimises for qualified starts of a ten-minute quickstart, and a team that
   * needs server-side moderation will discover this either way — the only
   * variable is whether they discover it here or after the ten minutes, and
   * only one of those two outcomes is repairable.
   *
   * It said "if your product needs any of that" for one round, and that was
   * too wide. A fresh reader building a marketplace pointed out that
   * client-side reporting — the user's own device attaches the plaintext to a
   * report — is a solved pattern, so the page was disqualifying products it
   * actually fits, in the last sentence they would read before closing the
   * tab. The condition is server-side capability, not the capability itself,
   * and the sentence now says so. It stops there deliberately: the SDK ships
   * no reporting API, and describing the client-side pattern as though it
   * were one would trade an over-disqualification for an invented feature.
   */
  {
    id: 'what-you-give-up',
    title: 'What you give up',
    body: 'Your relay holds ciphertext, so nothing on your backend can search message contents, moderate them, or produce them for a legal request. The SDK ships encrypted device-to-device transfer, but restoring history to a user who has lost every device is a backup you design. If any of that has to happen on your server, encrypt in transit and at rest instead — this is the wrong tool, and it is cheaper to know now.',
  },
];

/*
 * The runtime question is the first one a visitor has, and one quickstart per
 * runtime makes "does it run where I run" a click instead of a search. It sits
 * under the differentiator that raises it rather than in the hero, where three
 * more links were competing with the one action the hero is for.
 */
const runtimes = [
  { href: 'https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/expo', label: 'Expo' },
  { href: 'https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/browser', label: 'Browser' },
  { href: 'https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/node', label: 'Node' },
];

/*
 * Everything a reader can check, in the band that exists to be checked. The
 * security model and the review pack used to sit in a third row of links in
 * the hero, where they read as afterthoughts to the CTA rather than as the
 * page's answer to "prove it".
 */
const trustLinks = [
  { href: '/security', label: 'What the relay can and cannot see' },
  { href: '/evaluate', label: 'The security review pack' },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js/blob/main/docs/SECURITY.md',
    label: 'The security model and threat model',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md',
    label: 'Architecture and protocol profile',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js',
    label: 'View the SDK on GitHub',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="OpenE2EE"
  description="Add end-to-end encrypted messaging to a TypeScript app. The Signal Protocol in one npm package for Expo, React Native, browsers, and Node — your relay never needs message plaintext or device private keys."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        {/* "Add …" rather than naming the thing. The noun phrase described a
            category and left the reader to work out their own relationship to
            it; the verb states the job, and the job is the reason anyone is
            here — this goes into an app that already exists. It also puts the
            headline and the meta description into the same words, which they
            were not before. */}
        <h1>
          Add <span class="keep-together">end-to-end</span> encrypted messaging to your TypeScript
          app.
        </h1>
        {/* "Pure TypeScript" is the widest form of a scoped claim, and it is
            load-bearing that the scope is stated elsewhere. docs/messaging.md
            §5 approves the adjective of the *protocol code*; one level down the
            encrypted Expo store is expo-sqlite with SQLCipher and needs a
            development build. As its own sentence the phrase reads across the
            whole package, so the exception in the feature band below is not a
            footnote to it — it is the condition on it, and
            `tests/site-content.test.mjs` fails if the lead makes this claim
            while the page drops the exception.

            No runtime list and no adapter ownership in this paragraph. Both
            are on the page — the runtime links, the maturity line, the
            disclosure list — and the second line of the page is the position
            that decides whether a reader keeps reading, which is too expensive
            to spend on a compatibility matrix. The meta description carries the
            four names for a reader arriving from a search result.

            "Fully open source" took the slot "Drop-in E2EE" had, and the trade
            is deliberate. "Drop-in" was the only word here promising an easy
            integration, but the headline one line above already makes that
            promise in more words than a hyphen can — "Add … to your TypeScript
            app" is a verb about an app that already exists — and "Batteries
            included" carries the rest of it. What the page had nowhere above
            the fold was the licence, which is the first thing a developer
            evaluating a dependency wants and the one answer no competitor in
            this category can copy. "Fully" is the load-bearing word, and it is
            not decoration: the feature band below states that every feature is
            AGPL-3.0 with nothing time-limited, which is what makes the adverb
            true rather than promotional.

            Three fragments, three marks: one per clause, or the pattern reads
            as an accident. All three are now drawings rather than emoji. ⚡ went
            first — a bolt reads "fast" beside a clause that is not about speed,
            and TypeScript has a mark of its own that states the claim instead
            of decorating it. 🔋 followed, because one emoji left among two real
            marks is the accident rather than the pattern; `BatteryMark.astro`
            is drawn here, since "batteries included" is a figure of speech
            about defaults and nobody owns a mark for it.

            Only the keyhole is exposed to assistive technology, and only
            because it is a trademark drawn under conditions, one of which is
            that it links to opensource.org. The other two are aria-hidden:
            "TypeScript" and "batteries" are the nouns they stand before, so
            announcing them would repeat the sentence. See
            `src/components/OsiMark.astro` for the conditions, and for why that
            artwork is the solid mark rather than the outline one that shipped
            for a round and read as a smudge at this size. */}
        <p class="lead">
          {/* Keep each mark and the words around it on one line. Astro deletes
              the whitespace between a text run and an inline element, so
              wrapping this paragraph at a tag boundary silently joins a
              sentence to the mark after it — it rendered "Pure TypeScript.🔋"
              once. Where a line does have to break, the separator has to come
              back as an explicit {' '}. `tests/site-content.test.mjs` checks
              both edges of all three marks in the built HTML. */}
          <OsiMark /> Fully Open Source. <TypeScriptMark /> Pure TypeScript.{' '}
          <BatteryMark /> Batteries included.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          {/* No sublabel under this button. "ten minutes · two clients · no
              account" is still under the two further down the page, where a
              reader who has read the evidence is being asked to commit; here
              it was a third line of small grey type between the offer and the
              proof, and the proof is the better argument for spending ten
              minutes than the words "ten minutes" are. */}
          <a class="cta-primary" href="https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/quickstart">
            <span class="oe-button">Build your first encrypted message</span>
          </a>
          <a class="oe-button oe-button-secondary" href="https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js">
            <Icon name="github" />
            View the SDK on GitHub
          </a>
        </div>
        {/* Below the actions, never above them. The hero headline's floor is
            33px specifically because at 390px that is the difference between a
            three-line and a four-line headline, and it lands the primary button
            at 654 against a real-device fold of 664 — ten pixels of margin.
            Anything inserted between the lead and the button spends that margin
            and puts the page's one primary action under the fold, which is an
            acceptance gate rather than a preference.

            The runtime question is the first one a visitor has, and it was
            answered until now only by three links two screens down and a
            sentence in the feature band. This answers it where it is asked
            without bidding against the offer. */}
      </div>

      {/* A grid child rather than the last thing in `.hero-copy`, so it takes
          the container's full width like the install command and the code panel
          under it instead of the copy column's 48rem reading measure. Seven
          entries want more room than a paragraph does.

          No caveat paragraph under it. The experimental split is the maturity
          line's job below, and the S3 qualification is carried by the heading
          plus the entry's name — see `src/lib/platform-marks.mjs`. Both are
          test-pinned to the installed SDK. */}
      <PlatformStrip />

      {/* The demo sits between the offer and the objections now, rather than
          beside the copy in a second column.

          A column is the wrong thing to size a program to. The panel was the
          wide half of a 4.75/7.25 split — 645px — and the longest line of the
          ten variants is 91 characters, so the code had to be set at 13px by a
          desktop-only exception to make the two meet. Down the middle the
          panel is sized to the program instead: it takes whatever the code
          needs, and the size exception now runs the other way. The panel is
          52rem because 91 characters at 15px paint 754px, not the other way
          about; global.css states the arithmetic where the rule is.

          It has to come before the objections rather than after them. What a
          reader is deciding here is whether to spend ten minutes, and the
          question they ask first is what the API looks like — the two-column
          layout answered that for free by putting the panel level with the
          headline, and a single column only keeps it if the panel comes
          before two paragraphs of comparison. */}
      <div class="hero-demo">
        <HeroSnippet />
        {/* The caption said "lifted from the quickstart as recorded", which was
            true of the excerpt and is not true of this. Five of the thirteen
            non-blank lines differ, all for one reason: the recording drives an
            `alice` and a `bob` in a single process to show a round trip, and
            an application owns one client. Claiming "as recorded" over a
            renamed program is the kind of small overclaim that costs the whole
            page its credit when a reader opens /product and diffs it — which
            is exactly what the link invites them to do. So the caption now
            says what the reader is looking at, and the link still says where
            the unedited thing is. */}
        <p class="muted">
          Send and receive, as an application writes it.{' '}
          <a href="/product">See the whole recorded file.</a>
        </p>
        {/*
          The disclosure was one clause at the end of the caption above, and it
          named Bob's client and the device registration. Four fresh readers
          sized up the excerpt; every one of them found something it did not
          name. `inMemoryStore()` stands where a line item goes in the reader's
          own estimate — a real store, per device, per platform, and the thing
          whose "persisted formats may change before 1.0". `relay` is a server
          somebody has to run. Those are the two line items that decide whether
          this is a two-week or a two-month job, and they were the two the
          disclosure left out, which is worse than disclosing nothing: a reader
          who finds the omission themselves stops believing the parts you did
          admit.

          It is a list because a list can be checked against the code beside
          it, and a trailing clause cannot. It sits here because this is where
          the measured space was — two readers independently put a rectangle
          around the same emptiness under the caption, one calling it 630×137
          CSS px at 1280 and the other 569×238 at 1440, and both said the
          question the excerpt raises is asked exactly where nothing answers
          it. Every item is already on the page: two are in the code, one is in
          the lead, one was already in the caption.
        */}
        <div class="excerpt-drops">
          <p class="excerpt-drops-title">Not in this example, and yours to supply</p>
          {/* The bullets name the shipped adapter as well as the obligation,
              because naming only the obligation created a contradiction.

              "The SDK ships adapters for both" used to be in the lead, four
              blocks above "yours to supply" and 450 px to the left of it while
              the hero still had two columns, and a fresh reader put the two
              side by side and could not tell which was true: do I install an
              adapter and configure it, or do I stand up a network service
              before one real message moves? Their words — the difference
              between an afternoon and a sprint, and the only question that
              decides whether I try this today.

              The lead no longer says it, which is what makes these bullets the
              only statement of it. They were always the fuller one — the
              obligation and the shipped export in the same line — so the
              sentence went rather than the list.

              Both sentences were true and the pair was not. The store and the
              relay are yours to supply; the code for them ships. `ADAPTERS.md`
              lists `expoStore`, `nodeStore`, `indexedDbStore` and
              `reactNativeStore`, and `convexRelay` alongside `inMemoryRelay` — all
              exports of the installed package the build audit checks names
              against. So the answer is deploy, not write, and it took naming
              them to say so.

              The relay gets a definition because three fresh readers in one
              wave made the same complaint independently: it is the
              load-bearing noun of the whole pitch, it appears five times in
              the first viewport, and the page never says what it is. Each
              inferred "a server" and then could not size it. The description
              is the SDK's own — public keys, device lists, encrypted envelope
              delivery.

              What it deliberately does not say is what the relay can see. One
              reader proposed "it never sees message content or your keys",
              which is the absolute `design/DESIGN.md:403` forbids. The
              `.honesty` paragraph already carries the fixed formula and the
              metadata admission, one block below. A second, looser statement
              of the same boundary is how the loose one ends up quoted. */}
          <ul>
            <li>
              a real store in place of <code>inMemoryStore()</code>, on each device — <code
                >expoStore()</code
              >, <code>nodeStore()</code> and <code>indexedDbStore()</code> ship
            </li>
            <li>
              the relay <code>inMemoryRelay()</code> stands in for &mdash; it holds public keys and
              device lists and delivers the encrypted envelopes. <code>convexRelay()</code> ships
              one; you host it.
            </li>
            {/* These two used to read "registering both devices with it" and
                "Bob's own client, which is Alice's four lines renamed". Both
                were artefacts of quoting a recording that drives both sides of
                a conversation inside one process: the registration calls are
                `inMemoryRelay`'s own API, and the second client existed so the
                recording could show a round trip without a network. Neither is
                work the reader does, so listing them as work they owe was
                inflating the estimate the list exists to make honest. What is
                genuinely missing is the counterparty. */}
            <li>
              the other side &mdash; <code>"bob"</code> is another device running this same code, against
              the same relay
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hero-objections">
        {/*
          These paragraphs answer objections, and they follow both the offer and
          the demo rather than standing between the reader and either.

          A phone is why they came down here at all. 390×844 is the device
          height, not the viewport: an iPhone serves about 664 CSS px on first
          paint, and everything the reader is being asked to decide on has to
          arrive inside that. Measured at 390 today: headline 105–216, lead
          240–346, buttons 370–474, install command 506–585, and the code panel
          opening on 597. Five blocks, and the fifth is the evidence — 67 px of
          the program is above the fold rather than the 41 px it was, which is
          the whole of what the lead's third line was costing.

          It cost two lines to get there. The alpha and licence disclosure used
          to be the third block and the promise under the button the fourth, and
          together they pushed the button to 654 — ten pixels of clearance, with
          the disclosure's rule landing on 661, so the last row of pixels a
          reader saw was a full-width line that means "this section has ended".
          The promise is now under the two buttons further down the page and the
          disclosure is the last paragraph in this group.

          Moving it is not dropping it: `messaging.md` §1.2 asks for the limit in
          the same breath as the capability, and the sharpest breath on this page
          is not a grey line under the lead but the Store picker itself, where
          the two runtimes that are not ready are labelled "(experimental)" on
          the options that offer them. That reads at the moment of choosing. The
          paragraph below carries the rest — the version, the format warning, the
          licence — for the reader who scrolls, which is every reader who is
          still deciding.

          These paragraphs still start at 1739 on that phone and 1230 at 1440,
          behind the demo, and that ordering is deliberate. The lead names the
          alternative above the fold — "Not a hosted chat service, and more than
          TLS" — and what they add is the argument rather than the name. The
          proof goes first and the argument waits behind it.

          The libsignal sentence: three fresh readers answered every question
          the first viewport is for except this one, each giving the same
          reason — the page named no alternative, so "no native modules" was a
          contrast with nothing on the other side of it. Its own README is the
          strongest evidence available and it is one line long. The runtime
          clause repeats verbatim in the comparison band below, which is why
          the hero can afford to carry it and the fold can afford to lose it.
        */}
        {/* Twelve fresh readers have now written out the shortlist they hold
            before the page speaks, and every one of them tops it with the same
            option: ship it on TLS and encryption at rest, revisit E2EE later.
            The lead names that option — "and more than TLS" — and three
            readers in one wave independently returned the same verdict on it:
            "four words, no threat named", "an assertion, not an argument",
            "the entire argument against my number-one option". Naming an
            alternative is not arguing against it.

            The argument is in docs/positioning.md §5, where readable-data
            exposure is ranked the strongest adoption trigger of the eight, and
            its wording is deliberate: "end-to-end encryption can limit the
            readable content that an attacker gets from a breached relay." It
            says *can limit*, not prevents, and it is about a breached relay
            rather than about compliance — the same section opens by stating
            that almost no regulation requires E2EE at all. Reproduced close to
            verbatim here for that reason, hedge included.

            It sits above the libsignal sentence because the shortlists are
            ordered and libsignal is never first. It cannot go in the lead: the
            lead is two sentences and one of them already carries this
            argument's headline, and at 390 the real fold is 664 px with the
            code panel opening on 597. A third sentence up there buys the
            argument by spending the evidence, which is the trade this hero
            exists to refuse — and the 26 px the lead just gave back went to
            the program, not to another clause. */}
        <p class="stance">
          Why not just TLS? TLS protects a message on the wire and at-rest encryption protects the
          disk &mdash; in between, your server holds it readable. End-to-end encryption can limit
          the readable content a breached relay gives up.
        </p>
        <p class="stance">
          Why not libsignal? The reference implementation&rsquo;s own README says{' '}
          <a href={libsignalReadme.href}>&ldquo;use outside of Signal is unsupported&rdquo;</a>, and
          it does not run in Expo, React Native, or a browser.
        </p>
        {/* "we document exactly what" promised a document and pointed at
            nothing, in the one paragraph where a sceptical reader most wants
            to click through — a claim that cannot be checked at the moment it
            is being weighed. `/security` is where pricing, learn and product
            already send a reader for this same fact. */}
        <p class="honesty">
          The relay never needs message plaintext or device private keys. It still sees metadata —{' '}
          <a href="/security">we document exactly what</a>, and what sealed sender does and
          doesn&rsquo;t hide.
        </p>
        {/* The disqualifying facts, at the foot of the hero rather than
            between the lead and the button.

            They stood directly above the offer for eleven rounds, on the
            argument that a reader who reaches the button has not necessarily
            read down to a footnote. What that argument left out is the cost:
            three lines of grey metadata, with a rule over them, between the
            sentence that makes the offer and the button that takes it. The
            rule read as a section ending — one mobile critic recorded it
            landing on 661 px, the last row of pixels a phone paints — so the
            page closed itself immediately before asking for the click.

            They cannot leave the page, and they do not. docs/messaging.md §1.2
            puts the limit in the same breath as the claim, and §4 fixes
            "alpha" as the word; a test pins both, and pins them on the built
            page rather than on this file so that a comment quoting them cannot
            stand in for printing them. Moving them past the demo means the
            reader meets them having seen what the API looks like, which is the
            order the rest of the hero already follows: offer, proof, then the
            arguments and the limits.

            "; Expo and Node are not" closes an inference five separate readers
            made for themselves. One kind read "Browser and bare React Native
            stores are experimental" as *browsers are experimental*, harsher
            than the facts; the other kind reasoned by elimination and then
            reported having constructed the answer rather than read it.
            `ADAPTERS.md` marks the web and react-native stores
            `(experimental)` and puts no marker on the Expo or Node ones. "Are
            not" rather than "are complete" or "are production-ready": an
            absent marker is evidence for the absence of a marker, not for a
            capability grade, and the alpha clause in front of it governs all
            four.

            This was the only place the page graded the runtimes. The platform
            strip now qualifies its own two experimental entries inline, because
            a uniform row of marks two screens above this line was contradicting
            it for anyone who scanned and left. That is the same fact in the
            same notation, attached to the entry rather than restated as prose —
            the wording here stays the canonical one, and this is still the only
            place the page grades them in a sentence.

            The licence sentence links because three fresh readers were told
            three times that they owe money and never once what it costs, and
            because four others read "Pricing" in the nav against "no account"
            in the button and concluded a hosted service was being hidden. It
            sells a commercial licence, and /pricing carries the numbers. */}
        <p class="maturity">
          0.1.x alpha — public APIs and persisted formats may change before 1.0. Browser and bare
          React Native stores are experimental; Expo and Node are not. Free under AGPL-3.0; <a
            href="/pricing">commercial licence for closed source</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="band-head">
